# Java method length constraints

## Requirement

Java methods and constructors MUST contain no more than **60 effective source lines**. A justified exception requires explicit approval and recorded QA evidence.

## Counting rule

Count lines within the method body containing executable statements, declarations or structural syntax (including braces). Ignore blank lines, comment-only lines, and annotations outside the method body. Count a statement spanning multiple physical lines once per nonblank, non-comment physical line. A `case` line and a brace line count as effective lines. The method signature and its opening brace line count as one effective line. The closing brace counts as one line. A formatter is not authoritative for length checking; a suitable static analyzer or documented parser-based checker MUST enforce this definition.

## Guidance

Do not fragment cohesive operations solely to satisfy a numeric limit. Enforcement MUST apply the counting rule above consistently, including for generated and test code.
